narrative:
## Comparing Structs with Eq

By default, you cannot use `==` to compare two structs. Use `derive[Eq]` to tell the compiler to generate comparison logic automatically.

### Without Eq (Error)

```xiom
pub type Point = { x: Int; y: Int; }

let a = Point{ x: 1, y: 2 };
let b = Point{ x: 1, y: 2 };
// a == b;  // ERROR: Point does not implement Eq
```

### With Eq

```xiom
pub type Point = { x: Int; y: Int; } derive[Eq]

let a = Point{ x: 1, y: 2 };
let b = Point{ x: 1, y: 2 };
let same = a == b;   // true
let diff = a != b;   // false
```

### How Eq Works

`derive[Eq]` compares structs **field by field**. Two structs are equal if all their fields are equal:

```xiom
pub type Person = { name: Str; age: Int; } derive[Eq]

let alice = Person{ name: "Alice", age: 30 };
let bob = Person{ name: "Bob", age: 30 };
let also_alice = Person{ name: "Alice", age: 30 };

alice == bob;          // false — name differs
alice == also_alice;   // true — both fields match
```

### Eq in Control Flow

```xiom
fn greet(user: &Person) -> Str {
  let admin = Person{ name: "Root", age: 0 };
  if user == &admin {
    return "Welcome, Administrator!";
  }
  return "Welcome, " + user.name;
}
```

### Nested Structs with Eq

All field types must also implement Eq:

```xiom
pub type Address = { city: Str; } derive[Eq]
pub type Person = { name: Str; addr: Address; } derive[Eq]
```

